  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The coastal flood risk will linger into early next \nweek.\n\nAs a nor'easter churns offshore of the Eastern Seaboard, steady \nnortherly flow will continue across the Lowcountry and southeast \nGeorgia through the evening, before turning more west-northwesterly \ntonight. Even though this will create a less favorable environment \nfor coastal flooding, the astro tide will still be elevated given \nthe recent full moon. Thus, this will still result in minor \n(possible moderate) coastal flooding with each high tide cycle \nthrough early next week (esp. for the Charleston Harbor).\n\nCharleston Harbor: With the ongoing environmental conditions, tidal \nlevels could reach 7.4 ft MLLW in the Charleston Harbor for \nthis evening's high tide (approx. at 834 PM). Therefore, a \nCoastal Flood Advisory remains in effect through 11 PM this \nevening for Charleston and Colleton Counties. Saltwater inundation\nwill be possible 2 to 3 hours after high tide, and will likely \nresult in some roads becoming impassable.\n\nCoastal flooding concerns will continue tomorrow (9/27) with the \npotential to reach moderate flood stage for Charleston and Colleton \nCounty with the morning high tide (~9AM). Thereafter, it's likely to \nsee minor coastal flooding conditions through early next week for \nthe Charleston Harbor.\n\nFort Pulaski: Tidal levels could reach 9.2 ft MLLW for this \nevening's high tide in Fort Pulaski (approx. at 841PM), thus \nthis will not warrant a Coastal Flood Advisory for lower South \nCarolina and southeast Georgia. Additionally, guidance indicates\ntomorrow morning's high tide could see minor coastal flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rising temperatures through Tuesday, remaining above \nnormal next week. \n\nOne last fall-like afternoon as the nor-easter continues to move \ninto the northeast region, with rather tranquil conditions \npersisting for the next couple days over the southeast. Clear skies \nand light winds will set up another radiational cooling event \ntonight, with overnight lows in the lower to mid 50s for inland \nareas, and in the upper 50s to lower 60s for those along the coast. \n\nRising temperatures next week will be the main story, which will \nprimarily be driven by the low-level (h925) thermal fields as \nridging builds aloft into mid-week. Expect highs on Sunday primarily \nin the lower 80s, though areas south of the I-16 corridor will be in \nthe mid 80s. Monday will be a touch warmer as the mid-80s build \nnorthwards and upper 80s become likely south of the I-16 corridor, \nwith widespread mid 80s to lower 90s on Tuesday. Normally, high \ntemperatures in the end of September are in the lower/mid 80s, \nfalling into the upper 70s by the middle of October...so enjoy the \nwarmer temperatures while you can? While warm temperatures remain, \nmoisture returns Thursday into Friday along the backside of a \nsubtropical high pressure centered well offshore over the Atlantic, \nbringing some low rain chances to return.",
